  "version": "1.0",
  "changes_html": "<h2>Changes for the July 16, 2026 Update</h2>\n<p>Since our April 2026 release, we have made three methodological changes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Fixed a model bug in rate increase measurement.</strong> In the prior release, baseline MFN tariffs\n  on some products were being counted as contributing to the above-baseline ETR increase that\n  feeds into the price model. We have corrected this model bug.</li>\n<li><strong>Refined our measurement of statutory tariff rates.</strong> The Tariff Rate Tracker now more closely\n  aligns HTS revisions with the dates policies took effect and incorporates updated modeling of\n  Section 232 tariffs, Section 301 exclusions, statutory exemption lists, USMCA preference use,\n  and the pre-2025 MFN baseline.</li>\n<li><strong>Replaced a flat avoidance assumption with an adjustment calibrated to observed collections.</strong>\n  Our previous model reduced projected tariff revenue by a uniform amount to account for evasion.\n  The updated model instead compares statutory rates with duties observed in Census trade data and\n  estimates the gap separately by sector and trading-partner group. This adjustment captures not\n  only evasion, but also exemptions, preference claims, tariff avoidance, and measurement error\n  that are not fully visible in the statutory rate record. It is applied upstream throughout the\n  model, including in estimates of prices, household costs, trade responses, and revenue.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Together, these changes lower our estimates of effective tariff rates, consumer-price effects, and\naverage household costs relative to our April release. Revenue changes less because the earlier\nmodel already included an avoidance adjustment in that calculation. Because the new calibration is\nbased on experience under the 2025–2026 tariff regime, we will continue to update it as more data\nbecome available.</p>",
